@* macOS: vectors and matrices.
\label{macmath}The Windows version computes with DirectXMath, which does not exist on macOS. Its
replacement is Apple's \texttt{simd} library, which offers vector types such as
|simd::float3| and |simd::float4x4| that the compiler maps to SIMD registers, with the
usual operators and functions (|simd::dot|, |simd::cross|, |simd::normalize|, \dots). The
same types exist, with the same names, in the Metal Shading Language, which makes them the
natural choice.

The program needs only a few functions of DirectXMath beyond what \texttt{simd} provides:
three matrix constructors and two transformations. They live in a small header.

